export const GUIDE = "<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width,initial-scale=1\">\n<title>Phone Guard: how it works</title>\n<style>\n:root{color-scheme:light dark;--bg:#fff;--fg:#1c1b1a;--mut:#6b6a66;--line:#d9d7d0;--acc:#185fa5;--card:#f6f5f1}\n@media(prefers-color-scheme:dark){:root{--bg:#1c1b1a;--fg:#ecebe6;--mut:#a3a199;--line:#3a3935;--acc:#85b7eb;--card:#262522}}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--bg);color:var(--fg);font:16px/1.6 system-ui,sans-serif}main{max-width:38rem;margin:0 auto;padding:16px}\nh1{font-size:22px;font-weight:500}h2{font-size:18px;font-weight:500;margin:28px 0 6px}a{color:var(--acc)}\n.card{background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:12px;padding:10px 16px;margin:10px 0}.mut{color:var(--mut);font-size:14px}\nol{padding-left:1.3rem}li{margin:6px 0}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%}td{border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding:6px 4px;vertical-align:top}td:first-child{font-weight:500;width:34%}\n</style></head><body><main>\n<p><a href=\"/\">Back to your phones</a></p>\n<h1>How Phone Guard works</h1>\n<p>Your family has a small private service in your own Cloudflare account. It holds the settings and passes them to the child's phone. Nobody else runs it or can see it.</p>\n<h2>Who does what</h2>\n<table>\n<tr><td>Your computer</td><td>Runs the setup tool once. It creates the service in your Cloudflare account and makes the codes.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Your Cloudflare account</td><td>Holds the settings. The free plan is enough.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Child's phone</td><td>Checks in about every 15 minutes and follows the timetable. If it loses internet it keeps the last settings.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Your own phone</td><td>Opens this page to approve phones, change the timetable and connect Spotify.</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h2>Adding a phone</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>On this page press <b>Add a phone</b>. You get a QR code, an address and a code. They work for about 10 minutes.</li>\n<li>On the child's phone open Phone Guard, press <b>Connect to the family companion</b>, scan the QR code or type the address and code.</li>\n<li>The child's phone shows 4 digits. Type them here under <b>New phone waiting</b> and press <b>Approve</b>. This proves you are approving the phone in your hand.</li>\n<li>Write down the recovery key shown. It appears once and lets you take back control if you lose your private link.</li>\n<li>Within about 10 seconds the child's phone says it is paired.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Changing the timetable</h2>\n<p>Press <b>Timetable and settings</b> on a phone. Add a block for each part of the day, choose its mode, press <b>Save</b>. The phone picks it up at its next check-in, or sooner if you press <b>Sync now</b> on the phone. Times follow the time zone shown on the page, including clock changes.</p>\n<h2>What you can control</h2>\n<p>Open a phone from your page to find these (each is a section you can open):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Right now:</b> force a mode, give a temporary boost (up to 3 hours), set holiday dates.</li>\n<li><b>Timetable:</b> the weekly blocks, base mode and time zone.</li>\n<li><b>Apps and modes:</b> a tick grid of which apps each mode allows, approving new apps, asking the phone to remove an app.</li>\n<li><b>Mode settings:</b> screen, sound, calls, camera, location, notifications, radio, WhatsApp checks and more, per mode.</li>\n<li><b>Phone restrictions, WhatsApp, Radio stations, Apps on the Nokia menu, Weather place, Look of the phone.</b></li>\n<li><b>Spotify, Screen time, Battery and power saver, Updates and removals.</b></li>\n<li><b>Manage this phone:</b> rename, copy settings from another phone, release or forget it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"mut\">Changes reach the phone at its next check-in (about 15 minutes), or straight away if you press Sync now on the phone.</p>\n<h2>Spotify</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Go to developer.spotify.com/dashboard with a Premium account and create an app.</li>\n<li>Use the redirect address shown in the Spotify box on the phone's settings page, and tick Web API.</li>\n<li>Under Users and Access add the email of each Spotify account that will be used.</li>\n<li>Copy the Client ID into the Spotify box and press <b>Connect Spotify</b>. Sign in to Spotify when asked.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"mut\">The sign-in key is locked in your browser so that only the child's phone can open it. Your Cloudflare service only ever holds the locked version.</p>\n<h2 id=\"email\">Email</h2>\n<p>The child's phone can send and receive email with a normal mail account on its own simple screens. Use an address made for the child and an <b>app password</b> (a separate password just for this phone), not the normal password. Gmail, iCloud and Fastmail are filled in for you; another provider needs its incoming (IMAP) and outgoing (SMTP) server names. Microsoft addresses (Outlook.com, Hotmail, Live) do not work yet because Microsoft no longer accepts passwords for mail apps.</p>\n<p>Open the phone on your parent page, then <b>Email</b>, type the address and app password and press Save. The password is locked in your browser so only the child's phone can read it. The setup wizard on your computer can also test the login before sending it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"calendars\">Calendars</h2>\n<p>Family calendars can be shown on the phone, view only. For each one, copy its private iCal link (Google Calendar: Settings, the calendar, Integrate calendar, <b>Secret address in iCal format</b>), then on your parent page open the phone, <b>Calendars</b>, give it a name, paste the link and press Add. Anyone with the link can read that calendar, so the link is locked so only the child's phone can read it. Events the child adds on the phone stay on the phone.</p>\n<h2 id=\"whatsapp\">WhatsApp</h2>\n<p>This lets the child's phone read and send WhatsApp messages through its own simple screens, with the people you choose. Messages stay on the phone. The phone becomes a \"linked device\" of one WhatsApp account, the same way WhatsApp Web does. You need the smartphone that has that WhatsApp account (the child's own number if they have one, otherwise yours).</p>\n<ol>\n<li>On the <b>child's phone</b> open the pairing screen (Messages, then WhatsApp, or Settings, then WhatsApp; Messages only offers WhatsApp when the current mode allows WhatsApp checks). It is titled <b>Link WhatsApp</b> and asks for <b>Your WhatsApp number</b>: type it with the country code and no plus (for example 447700900123) and press <b>Get code</b>. The phone shows a code.</li>\n<li>On the <b>smartphone that has that WhatsApp account</b> open WhatsApp, then Settings (or the three dots), <b>Linked devices</b>, <b>Link a device</b>, then <b>Link with phone number instead</b>, and type the code from the child's phone.</li>\n<li>The child's phone says <b>Linked</b>. If the code runs out, press New code and try again.</li>\n<li>Choose who the child may message: open the phone on your parent page, then <b>WhatsApp</b>. Nobody is allowed until you add numbers (one per line, country code, no plus, or g: and a group name) or tick everyone.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><a href=\"whatsapp://\">Open WhatsApp on this phone</a> (WhatsApp cannot be opened straight onto its Linked devices screen, so follow the taps above).</p>\n<p class=\"mut\">WhatsApp can lapse a linked device if your main smartphone has not been online for about two weeks. If messages stop, link again from step 1.</p>\n<h2>Good to know</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Your private link opens this page already signed in. Anyone with it can manage the phones, so keep it private.</li>\n<li>To pair a phone again from scratch, use <b>Disconnect from the family companion</b> on its enrol screen.</li>\n</ul>\n</main></body></html>\n";
